// The single source of truth for every setting.
//
//   XPCOG_SETTING(Ident, Type, "cogKey", default)
//
// Keys are deliberately identical to Cog's NSUserDefaults keys, so an existing
// Cog preferences plist imports verbatim rather than resetting the user's setup.
// Cog has 126 of these; the ones the engine actually reads are listed here and
// the rest arrive with the features that consume them.
//
// This file is included several times with XPCOG_SETTING defined differently --
// an X-macro rather than a code generator, so there is no build step to keep in
// sync and the declarations cannot drift from the definitions.

// --- playback -------------------------------------------------------------

/// Which ReplayGain source to apply. One of: albumGain, albumGainWithPeak,
/// trackGain, trackGainWithPeak, soundcheck, volumeScale, none.
XPCOG_SETTING(VolumeScaling, std::string, "volumeScaling", "albumGainWithPeak")

/// Resampler quality passed to soxr: quick, low, medium, high, best.
///
/// Cog's key, deliberately with a different vocabulary. Cog's values name
/// *algorithms* (zoh, blep, linear, blam, cubic, sinc) because that is what its
/// own resampler offers; soxr has quality tiers instead, and inventing a second
/// key so both could coexist would leave a user setting the same idea twice.
/// libopenmpt's interpolation filter is chosen from these tiers -- see
/// codecs/openmpt.
XPCOG_SETTING(Resampling, std::string, "resampling", "high")

/// Fade on seek and stop, so transport changes do not click.
XPCOG_SETTING(EnableFading, bool, "enableFading", true)

/// Which device plays. Empty is the system default, and stays the system
/// default -- following it as the listener plugs in headphones, rather than
/// pinning whatever was default when the setting was written.
///
/// Cog stores a dictionary of name and CoreAudio device ID under `outputDevice`
/// and matches on either, rewriting the entry when the ID has moved
/// (Preferences/OutputsArrayController.m). Two keys here instead, because a
/// setting is a string: the id is what selects, the name is the fallback for
/// when it no longer resolves and is what the preferences pane can show for a
/// device that is currently unplugged.
XPCOG_SETTING(OutputDeviceId, std::string, "outputDeviceId", "")
XPCOG_SETTING(OutputDeviceName, std::string, "outputDeviceName", "")

/// Take the device exclusively, so nothing is mixed or resampled behind us.
///
/// **No Cog counterpart** -- Cog is CoreAudio only, where the equivalent is hog
/// mode and it does not offer one. What this buys is the device running at the
/// rate and format the player asked for rather than whatever the system mixer
/// is set to, which is the difference between a 96 kHz file being played and
/// being resampled to 48 on the way out.
///
/// Not every backend can: WASAPI can, and a backend that cannot falls back to
/// sharing rather than refusing to play. Off by default, because taking a
/// device exclusively stops every other sound on the machine.
XPCOG_SETTING(OutputExclusive, bool, "exclusiveOutput", false)

/// Decode HDCD control codes when present.
XPCOG_SETTING(EnableHDCD, bool, "enableHDCD", true)

/// Halve DSD on its way to PCM.
///
/// Cog's `halveDSDVolume`, off as it is there, and the default is the
/// interesting part. The decimation filter has a gain of 2.0 because DSD's
/// practical ceiling is about 50% modulation -- so 2.0 puts a hot SACD at full
/// scale instead of 6 dB below it, and the recordings that go higher are the
/// ones this exists for.
XPCOG_SETTING(HalveDsdVolume, bool, "halveDSDVolume", false)

/// Upmix stereo to 5.1 with FreeSurround. Cog's key and Cog's default.
///
/// Read once, when the device is opened: turning it on decides the device's
/// channel count, and that cannot change mid-stream without a gap.
XPCOG_SETTING(EnableFSurround, bool, "enableFSurround", false)

/// Emit DSD as DoP rather than decimating it to PCM. M6.
XPCOG_SETTING(OutputDSDAsDoP, bool, "outputDSDAsDoP", false)

/// Release the audio device while paused, so other applications can use it.
///
/// On -- the default, and what the output did unconditionally before this was
/// read -- pause() hands the device back. Off, it is held open and the callback
/// emits silence.
///
/// Cog reads it in OutputCoreAudio (:127, :1091); here the engine asks at each
/// pause rather than remembering it from start(), so unticking the box takes
/// effect on the next pause instead of the next track.
///
/// Holding the device is what someone chooses when reacquiring costs more than
/// it saves: an exclusive device another application would take in the gap, or a
/// DAC that clicks or drops its lock every time the stream stops. Suspending is
/// the polite default -- a paused player has no business holding a device
/// somebody else wants.
///
/// While held, the ring is not drained and the frame counter does not advance.
/// The second is the one that bites: that counter is the playback clock every
/// track change is timed against, so counting silence into it would walk the
/// seek bar forward through a pause.
XPCOG_SETTING(SuspendOutputOnPause, bool, "suspendOutputOnPause", true)

// --- output ---------------------------------------------------------------

/// Empty means the system default device.
XPCOG_SETTING(OutputDevice, std::string, "outputDevice", "")

/// Linear gain, 0.0 to 1.0. Cog stores this as a percentage; converted on import.
XPCOG_SETTING(Volume, double, "volume", 1.0)

// --- playlist -------------------------------------------------------------

/// Integers, not names, because Cog stores the raw enum value and the point of
/// matching its keys is that its plist imports without translation.
/// RepeatMode: 0 none, 1 one, 2 album, 3 all. ShuffleMode: 0 off, 1 albums, 2 all.
XPCOG_SETTING(RepeatMode, int, "repeat", 3)
XPCOG_SETTING(ShuffleMode, int, "shuffle", 0)
XPCOG_SETTING(AlwaysStopAfterCurrent, bool, "alwaysStopAfterCurrent", false)

/// When Next lands on a track that will not open, keep the current one playing
/// while the search for a playable entry runs.
///
/// **No Cog counterpart.** Cog's -next opens the entry it lands on and reports
/// the failure, so there is no search to have an opinion about.
///
/// Off, the search runs where Next has always run: what was playing stops, each
/// attempt is named in the status line, and each dead entry greys as it is
/// passed. On, nothing audible changes until a playable entry is found -- the
/// entries are opened and closed on the start thread while the track that is
/// playing carries on -- and if none is found, it simply keeps playing.
///
/// Off by default. A Next that leaves the same track playing looks, for the
/// second before anything is said, exactly like a Next that did nothing.
XPCOG_SETTING(KeepPlayingWhileSkipping, bool, "keepPlayingWhileSkipping", false)
/// Move the playlist selection to each track as it starts.
///
/// Cog's key, and a different question from PanelFollowMode below it: this moves
/// the *selection*, which is what the panes then follow by default. With both
/// on, "panels follow selection" tracks playback on its own until the listener
/// clicks something else.
///
/// Cog moves the selection as each next entry is chosen, from six places inside
/// -getNextEntry: and -getPrevEntry:. MainFrame does it once, as the track
/// becomes current: choosing the next entry is core's job and selecting a row is
/// the interface's, so there is no equivalent hook to hang six calls on.
XPCOG_SETTING(SelectionFollowsPlayback, bool, "selectionFollowsPlayback", false)

/// Start playing whatever was playing when the player last closed.
///
/// Cog's, off as it is there. The track itself is already restored either way --
/// the library records which entry was current, and the row is selected on
/// launch whatever this says, which is Cog's behaviour too
/// (AppController.m:285-290: select always, play only if this is set).
///
/// What this adds is starting it, at the position it stopped at, paused if that
/// is how it was left. See LastPlaybackStatus below for how "how it was left" is
/// remembered.
XPCOG_SETTING(ResumePlaybackOnStartup, bool, "resumePlaybackOnStartup", false)

/// How playback was last left: 0 stopped, 1 playing, 2 paused.
///
/// Cog's key and Cog's vocabulary (CogStatusStopped and friends). Not a
/// preference -- nothing offers it a control -- but it is Cog's key rather than
/// one of ours, so it belongs here beside the setting that reads it rather than
/// among the `xpcog.*` window state.
///
/// Stopped is what stops the previous session being resumed at all, which is why
/// it is written on every transport change rather than only at exit: a player
/// that crashed while stopped must not come back playing.
XPCOG_SETTING(LastPlaybackStatus, int, "lastPlaybackStatus", 0)

/// Take cue sheets found beside the audio when a folder is added.
///
/// **The default changed from true to false on 2026-08-23, to match Cog.** Cog
/// registers @NO (PlaylistLoader.m:70) and skips .cue while walking a folder
/// when it is off (:264-282), and the reason is duplication: a folder holding
/// album.cue beside album.flac otherwise adds every track twice, once through
/// the cue sheet and once as the whole file underneath it.
///
/// The old `true` was not a decision. Nothing read the setting at all, so the
/// scanner took every cue sheet it found, and `true` was the value that
/// described what already happened rather than one anybody chose. Someone who
/// wants both still gets both by ticking the box.
XPCOG_SETTING(ReadCueSheetsInFolders, bool, "readCueSheetsInFolders", false)

/// Take M3U and PLS files found in a folder as playlists to expand.
///
/// Cog's `readPlaylistsInFolders`, @NO as it is there and for the same reason as
/// the cue sheet setting above -- a folder holding its own .m3u adds everything
/// in it a second time.
///
/// Added with that one because Scanner::Options has carried `readPlaylists`
/// beside `readCueSheets` since it was written, and nothing could ever set it.
/// Half a pair of options where only one can be reached is the same dead wiring
/// the audit was looking for.
XPCOG_SETTING(ReadPlaylistsInFolders, bool, "readPlaylistsInFolders", false)

// --- pitch and tempo --------------------------------------------------------
//
// Cog's Rubber Band pane and its two main-window sliders. Keys are Cog's, so an
// imported plist carries a user's speed setup across; `varispeed` as an engine
// value is ours alone -- Cog has no resampling speed control, and a Cog plist
// can never hold it, so importing stays one-way safe.

/// Playback pitch as a frequency ratio, 0.2 to 5.0. 1.0 is unshifted.
XPCOG_SETTING(Pitch, double, "pitch", 1.0)

/// Playback tempo as a speed ratio, 0.2 to 5.0. 1.0 is unstretched.
XPCOG_SETTING(Tempo, double, "tempo", 1.0)

/// Move pitch and tempo together, which is what a turntable's speed control
/// does. Cog's `speedLock`, and on by default as it is there. Enforced by the
/// UI writing both keys rather than by the engine, again as in Cog.
XPCOG_SETTING(SpeedLock, bool, "speedLock", true)

/// Which time-stretch engine runs: disabled, varispeed, signalsmith, faster,
/// finer. Cog's key and Cog's default of disabled; `faster` and `finer` are
/// Rubber Band's R2 and R3 engines, `signalsmith` is Signalsmith Stretch, and
/// `varispeed` resamples -- pitch and tempo locked together by physics, in
/// trade for costing almost nothing.
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandEngine, std::string, "rubberbandEngine", "disabled")

// The Rubber Band option rows, vocabulary and defaults verbatim from Cog
// (Application/PlaybackController.m initDefaults). Each maps onto one
// RubberBandOption group in TimeStretch.cpp; the R3 engine reads only window,
// formant and transients, which is Cog's behaviour and Rubber Band's contract.
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandTransients, std::string, "rubberbandTransients", "crisp")
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandDetector, std::string, "rubberbandDetector", "compound")
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandPhase, std::string, "rubberbandPhase", "laminar")
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandWindow, std::string, "rubberbandWindow", "standard")
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandSmoothing, std::string, "rubberbandSmoothing", "off")
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandFormant, std::string, "rubberbandFormant", "shifted")
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandPitch, std::string, "rubberbandPitch", "highspeed")
XPCOG_SETTING(RubberbandChannels, std::string, "rubberbandChannels", "apart")

// --- equaliser ------------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether the equaliser runs at all -- Cog's `GraphicEQenable`, and its
/// default of off.
///
/// Not the same thing as a flat curve, which is why it exists. A flat equaliser
/// is already skipped rather than run, so 0 dB costs nothing; what this buys is
/// bypassing a curve *without* flattening it, which is the whole of what A/B-ing
/// one means. Zeroing 31 sliders to hear the track without them, and then
/// putting them back, is not a comparison anybody makes twice.
///
/// Off by default, as in Cog. A listener who has never opened the equaliser has
/// a flat curve, which is skipped either way, so the default is invisible until
/// somebody moves a slider -- at which point the panel turns it on for them,
/// because a slider that does nothing is worse than a checkbox they did not
/// ask for.
XPCOG_SETTING(GraphicEqEnable, bool, "GraphicEQenable", false)

/// Gain in dB applied ahead of the bands, to buy back headroom a boost costs.
XPCOG_SETTING(EqPreamp, double, "eqPreamp", 0.0)

/// The 31 band gains in dB, in the order Equalizer::bandFrequencies() reports.
///
/// One key per band rather than a single packed value because that is how Cog
/// stores them -- these names come from EqualizerWindowController's
/// `_cog_equalizer_band_settings()`, and keeping them means an existing Cog plist
/// carries a user's curve across verbatim instead of flattening it. The `p`
/// stands for a decimal point, again Cog's spelling.
///
/// Verbose, and worth it: the alternative is one string that has to be parsed,
/// which cannot be imported from Cog and cannot be edited one band at a time.
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq20Hz, double, "eq20Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq25Hz, double, "eq25Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq31p5Hz, double, "eq31p5Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq40Hz, double, "eq40Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq50Hz, double, "eq50Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq63Hz, double, "eq63Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq80Hz, double, "eq80Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq100Hz, double, "eq100Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq125Hz, double, "eq125Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq160Hz, double, "eq160Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq200Hz, double, "eq200Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq250Hz, double, "eq250Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq315Hz, double, "eq315Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq400Hz, double, "eq400Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq500Hz, double, "eq500Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq630Hz, double, "eq630Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq800Hz, double, "eq800Hz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq1kHz, double, "eq1kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq1p2kHz, double, "eq1p2kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq1p6kHz, double, "eq1p6kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq2kHz, double, "eq2kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq2p5kHz, double, "eq2p5kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq3p1kHz, double, "eq3p1kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq4kHz, double, "eq4kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq5kHz, double, "eq5kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq6p3kHz, double, "eq6p3kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq8kHz, double, "eq8kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq10kHz, double, "eq10kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq12kHz, double, "eq12kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq16kHz, double, "eq16kHz", 0.0)
XPCOG_SETTING(Eq20kHz, double, "eq20kHz", 0.0)

/// Which preset the current curve came from, as an index into the shipped
/// library -- Cog's `GraphicEQpreset`, and its default of -1.
///
/// Two values mean "no preset": -1, which is a curve that was never a preset,
/// and the library's size, which is the "Custom" row at the end of the selector
/// and is what moving any slider sets. Cog distinguishes them only by which one
/// it happens to write, and so does this.
///
/// An index rather than a name because that is what Cog stores, and the cost of
/// it is real: reorder the library file and every settings file that names a
/// preset now names a different one. Kept anyway, so that a preferences file
/// carried over from Cog selects the preset it selected there -- the same
/// bargain the band keys above make.
///
/// The stored curve is the record, not this. XPCog does not re-apply the preset
/// at startup the way Cog's equalizerLoadPreset() does, because the 32 band
/// settings already hold what the preset produced and re-deriving them would
/// only differ if the library file had changed underneath -- in which case
/// keeping the curve the user has been listening to is the better answer.
XPCOG_SETTING(GraphicEqPreset, int, "GraphicEQpreset", -1)

/// Follow each track's genre tag, choosing the preset whose name matches it.
///
/// Off by default, as in Cog, and worth understanding before turning on: a
/// track with no genre tag matches nothing, and Cog's fallback for matching
/// nothing is the preset named "Flat". So this does not mean "adjust when there
/// is something to adjust for" -- it means the equaliser is rewritten at every
/// track boundary, to flat if the tag is missing.
XPCOG_SETTING(GraphicEqTrackGenre, bool, "GraphicEQtrackgenre", false)

// --- appearance -----------------------------------------------------------

/// Which language the interface speaks. Empty follows the system.
///
/// A catalogue's code -- `es` -- or `en` for the language the strings are
/// written in, which loads no catalogue at all. Empty is the default and is not
/// the same as `en`: it means "whatever the desktop asks for", and it keeps
/// meaning that when the desktop's answer changes.
///
/// A code this build has no catalogue for is treated as empty rather than
/// honoured, so a settings file carried back from a build with more languages in
/// it falls back to the system's choice instead of to a language nothing here
/// can supply.
///
/// **No Cog counterpart.** Cog ships its own localisations and takes the
/// language from macOS's per-application preference, which is a system-level
/// setting rather than one of its own; there is no equivalent on Windows, so the
/// choice has to live here. Read once, by app/src/Localization.cpp, before any
/// window is built -- changing it relabels nothing that is already on screen,
/// which is what the note beside the row in Preferences says.
XPCOG_SETTING(Language, std::string, "language", "")

/// **Dead, and kept deliberately.** The Qt build let the listener choose a widget
/// style -- windows11, windowsvista, Fusion -- because Qt draws its own controls
/// and can therefore draw them several ways. wx uses the platform's, which is the
/// point of the move, and has no equivalent to offer; nothing reads this any more
/// and no pane draws a row for it.
///
/// It stays here so a settings file that travelled from a Qt build keeps its
/// value rather than having it dropped on the first save, and
/// PreferencesDialog's kCuratedKeys keeps it out of Advanced, where a key that
/// does nothing would be worse than no key at all. See docs/WXPORT.md.
///
/// The one setting here with **no Cog counterpart** -- Cog is macOS-only and has
/// exactly one look, so there was nothing to key against.
XPCOG_SETTING(WidgetStyle, std::string, "widgetStyle", "")

/// Closing the window leaves XPCog running in the notification area instead of
/// quitting. Off by default: changing what a close button does without being asked
/// is how an application gets a reputation for not closing.
///
/// **No Cog counterpart**, and it could not have one -- Cog is macOS-only, where
/// closing a window has never meant quitting the application. What this really
/// does is offer that same behaviour on the platforms whose convention is the
/// opposite.
///
/// Honoured only when a tray icon actually exists. Without one there is no way
/// back to a hidden window, so the setting is ignored rather than trapping the
/// application somewhere unreachable -- see StatusPresence::hasTrayIcon().
XPCOG_SETTING(CloseToTray, bool, "closeToTray", false)

/// Whether the "still playing, in the tray" notice has already been shown.
///
/// The notice exists because a window that vanishes with no explanation is
/// indistinguishable from a crash. That is a thing worth saying once, and only
/// once: someone who has closed to the tray twenty times knows where the window
/// went, and a toast on every close is the kind of nagging that teaches people
/// to dismiss notifications without reading them.
///
/// Recorded rather than kept in memory for the session, because the two are
/// different promises. Per-session means "once every launch", which for anyone
/// who quits daily is the same as always. This means once, ever.
///
/// **No Cog counterpart**, for the same reason CloseToTray has none.
/// Set back to false to see it again.
XPCOG_SETTING(TrayHideAnnounced, bool, "trayHideAnnounced", false)

// --- notifications --------------------------------------------------------

/// Announce each track as it starts. Cog's `notifications.enable`, and Cog's
/// default: **on**.
///
/// The one setting here whose default is on rather than off, and it is Cog's
/// call rather than a new one. A player that says what it just started is doing
/// the thing the listener asked for; the tray notice next door defaults to
/// showing once precisely because *it* is about the application rather than
/// about the music.
///
/// The keys carry Cog's dots. Every other key in this file is a flat
/// identifier, and SettingsStore.hpp asks that they stay that way -- but the
/// thing it asks for is no '/' or '=', because those are what the three stores
/// would have to escape. A '.' is a value name in the registry, a key in a
/// plist, and text before the first '=' in the file store, so it costs nothing.
/// Matching Cog exactly is worth more than tidiness in a file nobody reads by
/// hand: a preferences plist carried over from Cog brings the answer with it.
XPCOG_SETTING(NotificationsEnable, bool, "notifications.enable", true)

/// Put the cover in the notification, where the platform can show one.
///
/// Cog's `notifications.show-album-art`, on as it is there. Cog attaches the art
/// by writing it to a temp file, because UNNotificationAttachment takes a URL
/// (PlaybackEventController.m:190-200); wx takes a wxIcon, so nothing is written
/// to disk here.
///
/// Honoured where the platform's notification can carry an image and ignored
/// where it cannot, which is a real difference rather than a bug -- see
/// StatusPresence::notifyTrack().
XPCOG_SETTING(NotificationsShowAlbumArt, bool, "notifications.show-album-art", true)

/// **Not ported: `notifications.itunes-style`.** Cog reads it, defaults it to
/// true, and no longer offers it in the Notifications pane -- and what it
/// selects is a second notification style built on the deprecated
/// NSUserNotification path that exists alongside the UNUserNotificationCenter
/// one. There is one notification API here and no second style for a setting to
/// choose between, so a key for it would be a switch wired to nothing.

/// Which track the Info and Lyrics panes describe. 0 selection, 1 playback.
///
/// **No Cog counterpart**, and the default is Cog's behaviour so that it needs
/// none: 0 is the rule both of Cog's controllers implement -- the playlist
/// selection when there is one, the playing track otherwise
/// (InfoWindowController, LyricsWindowController.m:33-43).
///
/// 1 exists because that rule has a real failure mode once the panes are docked
/// rather than floating. Reading the lyrics of what is playing while arrowing
/// down the playlist looking for what to queue next changes the pane under you
/// on every keypress, and there is no way to say "no, keep showing that one".
/// Cog never had to answer this because its windows are opened *about* a track;
/// a pane that is always on screen is a different question.
///
/// Not three modes. "Selection only" would blank the panes whenever nothing is
/// selected -- clicking empty space, or removing the last row -- which is the
/// case the fallback in mode 0 exists to prevent, so it would be an option whose
/// whole content is a bug.
///
/// An int rather than a bool, following `repeat` and `shuffle` above: these are
/// modes chosen from a radio group in a menu, and the two that already work that
/// way are ints.
XPCOG_SETTING(PanelFollowMode, int, "panelFollowMode", 0)

// --- mini player ----------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether the mini player is showing instead of the main window. A mode, not a
/// second window: Cog's -setMiniMode: closes one and opens the other, and restores
/// it at launch from this key.
///
/// Written as the mode changes rather than on the way out, which is where Cog
/// writes it too (AppController.m:1027, inside -setMiniMode:). After a crash you
/// come back to the mode you were last in rather than the one you were in the
/// last time the application exited tidily.
///
/// Restored from a queued call rather than during construction: setMiniMode(true)
/// hides the frame, and XPCogApp::OnInit shows it after the constructor returns.
XPCOG_SETTING(MiniMode, bool, "miniMode", false)

/// Keep the mini player above other windows.
XPCOG_SETTING(FloatingMiniWindow, bool, "floatingMiniWindow", false)

// --- spectrum -------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Cog exposes five spectrum preferences. Three are ported and two are not:
// `spectrumSceneKit` ("Use 3D rendered spectrum") and `spectrumProjectionMode`
// ("Use flat perspective") both configure SpectrumViewSK, the SceneKit renderer.
// XPCog ports SpectrumViewCG, the Core Graphics one, so those two keys would
// describe a renderer that does not exist here -- carried but ignored is worse
// than absent, because a user who finds the checkbox expects it to do something.

/// Bar colour, as #RRGGBB. Cog's default is sRGB(1.0, 0.5, 0.0).
///
/// The key matches Cog's, but the *value* does not: Cog stores colours as
/// NSKeyedArchiver blobs through its ColorToValueTransformer, so an imported Cog
/// plist has an archived NSColor here rather than a string. That fails to parse
/// and falls back to this default, which is the same colour Cog defaults to -- so
/// the visible result of an import is correct for anyone who never changed it, and
/// a reset to orange for anyone who did. Decoding a keyed archive to do better is
/// not worth it for two colours.
XPCOG_SETTING(SpectrumBarColor, std::string, "spectrumBarColor", "#ff8000")

/// Peak marker colour, as #RRGGBB. Cog's default is the system red.
XPCOG_SETTING(SpectrumDotColor, std::string, "spectrumDotColor", "#ff3b30")

/// false: bands on musical notes, one bar per semitone (Cog's
/// DDB_ANALYZER_MODE_OCTAVE_NOTE_BANDS, and its default).
/// true: bars spaced evenly in log frequency (DDB_ANALYZER_MODE_FREQUENCIES).
XPCOG_SETTING(SpectrumFreqMode, bool, "spectrumFreqMode", false)

/// The quietest level drawn, in dB below full scale. Cog fixes this at -80
/// (LOWER_BOUND in SpectrumViewCG.m) with no way to change it.
///
/// **No Cog counterpart.** Exposed because the right value depends on the
/// material: -80 dB suits mastered pop, and buries the detail in something quiet
/// enough that the whole display sits in the bottom fifth of the range.
XPCOG_SETTING(SpectrumFloorDb, double, "spectrumFloorDb", -80.0)

/// Draw the held-peak markers above the bars.
///
/// **No Cog counterpart** -- Cog always draws them. A toggle because they are the
/// element most likely to be unwanted: they are what makes the display busy, and
/// the bars alone read as calmer.
XPCOG_SETTING(SpectrumShowPeaks, bool, "spectrumShowPeaks", true)

// --- synthesised formats --------------------------------------------------
//
// Modules and game-music rips are emulated rather than decoded, so they have no
// inherent sample rate and often no inherent length. Cog's keys and defaults,
// shared across every such decoder exactly as they are there.

/// Rendering rate for synthesised formats. Cog clamps to 8000..192000.
XPCOG_SETTING(SynthSampleRate, int, "synthSampleRate", 44100)

/// How long to play a rip that states no length and no loop. Cog's 2.5 minutes.
XPCOG_SETTING(SynthDefaultSeconds, double, "synthDefaultSeconds", 150.0)

/// Fade at the end of a synthesised track, which is what turns "loops for ever"
/// into something with a duration.
XPCOG_SETTING(SynthDefaultFadeSeconds, double, "synthDefaultFadeSeconds", 8.0)

/// How many times to play a rip's looping section when it states a loop but no
/// total length. Cog clamps to 0..10.
XPCOG_SETTING(SynthDefaultLoopCount, int, "synthDefaultLoopCount", 2)

/// Which synthesiser renders MIDI.
///
/// A MIDI file is a score, not a recording, so this setting decides what it
/// actually sounds like -- more than any other setting in this file. Cog's
/// `midiPlugin` key, and deliberately Cog's vocabulary too, so a settings file
/// carried over from Cog keeps naming the same synthesiser:
///
///   `DOOM0`..`DOOM5`  Nuked OPL3 through id's DMX driver, one per instrument
///                     bank -- DMX default, Doom 1, Doom 2, Raptor, Strife,
///                     DMXOPL.
///   `OPL3W0`          Nuked OPL3 through Nuke.YKT's General MIDI driver.
///   `Spessa`          SpessaSynth, playing the bank named by `soundFontPath`.
///   `NukeSc55`        A Roland SC-55, from the ROMs at `midiRomPath`.
///
/// `Spessa` is also what Cog migrates `BASSMIDI`, `FluidSynth` and `TinySF` to,
/// and those names are accepted here for the same reason -- a settings file
/// written by an older Cog names a SoundFont synthesiser that no longer exists
/// under a name that still means "play my bank". Cog additionally accepts a
/// four-plus-four character AudioUnit component code, which is macOS-only and
/// is not being ported. Anything unrecognised falls back to the default rather
/// than refusing to play.
///
/// The default is `Spessa`, as Cog's is, and for the reason Cog can afford it:
/// XPCog now ships a bank (assets/soundfonts, and shippedBank() in
/// codecs/midi/SoundFontSynth.hpp). This entry said the opposite until that
/// landed -- a Sound Blaster, "because it is the one synthesiser here that
/// needs nothing the listener has to supply" -- which was the right default
/// while it was true and stopped being true the moment there was a bank in the
/// box.
///
/// `NukeSc55` still wants a file nobody can be handed, and still falls back to
/// the OPL3 when it is not there.
XPCOG_SETTING(MidiPlugin, std::string, "midiPlugin", "Spessa")

/// Where the Roland SC-55 ROM set is, for the `NukeSc55` synthesiser.
///
/// Either the folder holding the five ROM files or the archive they arrived in;
/// both are read in place. **No Cog counterpart**, and the difference is
/// deliberate: Cog has an importer that copies them into its own application
/// support folder and then reads a fixed path, so the listener ends up with two
/// copies of 3.6 MB of commercial firmware and no say in where the second one
/// went. Naming the originals costs a settings row and takes nothing.
///
/// The files are identified by SHA-256 rather than by name, so a dump under
/// Roland part numbers works unrenamed. Empty, or pointing at something that is
/// not a complete set of one model, falls back to the OPL3 -- the file still
/// plays, and the track properties say what rendered it.
XPCOG_SETTING(MidiRomPath, std::string, "midiRomPath", "")

/// Which SoundFont SpessaSynth plays.
///
/// Cog's `soundFontPath` key, and the same meanings: a `.sf2`, `.sf3`,
/// `.sf2pack` or `.dls` bank, or an `.sflist`/`.json` naming several with the
/// programs and channels each covers. Read in place, and streamed rather than
/// loaded -- a bank can be well over a gigabyte, and a piece of music reaches
/// almost none of it.
///
/// A file may also bring its own bank, which wins over this one, and there are
/// two ways it can: inside itself if it is an RMID, or beside itself under one
/// of three names. See embeddedBank() in codecs/midi/MidiFile.hpp and
/// findCompanionBank() in codecs/midi/SoundFontSynth.hpp.
///
/// Empty means the bank XPCog ships rather than no bank, which is why this
/// defaults to empty rather than to a path: a path would be wrong on the next
/// machine, and would also stop the listener ever getting an updated bank from
/// an updated build. Only a build with no assets beside it -- which should not
/// happen and is not fatal -- falls back to the OPL3, and the track properties
/// say what rendered it either way.
XPCOG_SETTING(SoundFontPath, std::string, "soundFontPath", "")

/// Ring size for the HTTP source, in bytes. Must be a power of two between
/// 64 KiB and 128 MiB; anything else falls back to the default. A slow or
/// distant station is the reason to raise it.
XPCOG_SETTING(HttpStreamingBufferSize, int, "httpStreamingBufferSize", 0x40000)

/// Recently opened URLs, newest last, newline-separated and capped at fifteen.
///
/// Cog's `UserDefaultURLsKey`, same name and same cap (OpenURLPanel.m:57). Cog
/// stores a real array; settings here are typed scalars, and one string is a
/// smaller thing to add than a list type used exactly once. A newline is an
/// unambiguous separator because a URL cannot contain a raw one -- it would be
/// percent-encoded -- so nothing has to be escaped on the way in or out.
XPCOG_SETTING(UrlHistory, std::string, "UserDefaultURLsKey", "")

// --- scrobbling -----------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether plays are reported to Last.fm.
///
/// Cog's `enableAudioScrobbler`, same key -- but **deliberately not imported**
/// from a Cog plist, which is the one place `settings.def` sharing Cog's
/// spellings does not mean the value comes across. See `neverImported()` in
/// core/src/library/CogImport.cpp for the reasoning; the short version is that
/// the credential cannot follow the switch, so importing the switch alone would
/// show scrobbling as enabled with nothing behind it.
///
/// **Off by default, and Cog's own default is the interesting part.** Cog
/// registers this as `@YES` (PlaybackEventController.m:39) and then gates every
/// call on `lastFM.isAuthenticated` besides, so a fresh Cog is "enabled" and
/// silent. That arrangement makes the checkbox mean something different before
/// and after connecting, which is exactly the sort of thing this port has been
/// taking back out -- and it is also why importing it would carry no
/// information: "on" in a Cog plist mostly just means nobody turned it off.
/// Off here, turned on by connecting, and the pane says which of the two is
/// missing when neither is true.
///
/// The session key itself is deliberately **not** a setting. It is a credential,
/// it belongs in the platform's secret store, and settings here are world-
/// readable text in a registry key or a plist. See app/src/LastFmAccount.hpp.
XPCOG_SETTING(EnableScrobbling, bool, "enableAudioScrobbler", false)

// --- crash reporting ------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether the listener has agreed to send crash reports and usage data.
///
/// Cog's `sentryConsented`, same key and same default, and the default is the
/// whole point: **off**. Nothing is initialised, nothing is written to disk and
/// no request is made until this is true. Cog starts the SDK from the observer
/// on this key and calls `[SentrySDK close]` when it goes back to false
/// (Application/AppController.m:384-425); XPCog does the same through
/// platform::startCrashReporting() and stopCrashReporting().
///
/// A false stored here and a missing value mean the same thing on purpose. An
/// upgrade that added the key must not read "not asked yet" as "yes".
XPCOG_SETTING(SentryConsented, bool, "sentryConsented", false)

/// Whether the consent prompt has already been shown.
///
/// Cog's `sentryAskedConsent` (Window/MainWindow.m:19-38), and Cog's promise
/// with it: asked exactly once, ever, and the flag is written whether the answer
/// was yes, no, or the window being closed. Recorded rather than kept for the
/// session for the same reason TrayHideAnnounced is -- per-session would
/// mean "on every launch", which for a consent prompt is nagging.
///
/// Set it back to false to see the prompt again.
XPCOG_SETTING(SentryAskedConsent, bool, "sentryAskedConsent", false)

// --- bookkeeping ----------------------------------------------------------

/// Bumped by SettingsMigration; see Settings::applyMigrations().
XPCOG_SETTING(SettingsSchemaVersion, int, "settingsSchemaVersion", 0)
